Skill content under a --deny-dir target was reaching the provider request. I measured it, fenced the skill loader, and committed (6e853815). I also posted the results table on #1002.

What I measured. I ran the real binary against a stub provider that saves every request body it receives. I planted a marker string (SKILL_CANARY_ZZ7) in a skill's description and counted how often it appeared in the request:

| skill setup | no fence | --deny-dir (before → after) | --allow-dir proj |
|---|---|---|---|
| .yoyo/skills symlinked to secret/skills | 1 | 1 → 0 | 1 |
| plain .yoyo/skills/canary | 1 | 1 → 0 | – |
| --skills <secret>/skills | 1 | 1 → 0 | – |

• The no-fence column is the control that proves this check can see a leak. Days 221/222 got 0 hits from --print-system-prompt even for unfenced skills; that wasn't proof of safety, it just can't see skills.
• I didn't run the old binary for the --skills "before" number. It comes from the same check with the fence forced to let everything through, which behaves the same as the old loader.
• Only each skill's name, description and file location leaked. The body of the skill file is never sent.

The fix
• **Where:** a new `src/skill_fence.rs` checks every skill directory with the same path check the file tools and instruction loader use (`DirectoryRestrictions::check_path), judging symlinks by where they point. It runs before yoagent reads anything, and covers --skills, .yoyo/skills/, ~/.yoyo/skills/` and the install dir.
• **Order change:** I moved the code that reads the directory fence settings above skill loading. Project-context fencing is unchanged; only the order of that code's own stderr warnings moved.
• **Whole directories, not single skills:** yoagent's `SkillSet` can't have skills removed once loaded. So if any skill in a directory is refused, the whole directory is skipped, and a normal (not dimmed, not quiet-gated) warning names every refused file.
• **Behaviour change for users:** with `--allow-dir`, a `~/.yoyo/skills/` outside every allowed directory is now refused. `read_file` already refused those skills' file locations, so the model was being offered skills it couldn't open.

Verification
• **Pass-through:** with --allow-dir proj, or a deny entry somewhere else, the request is byte-identical to the unfenced one.
• **Positive control:** I forced the fence to let everything through, ran the tests and restored it, all in one command. Both deny tests failed by name, then passed again; no neutered marker was left in the code.
• **Checks:** build, tests (20 suites, 0 failed), clippy with warnings as errors, fmt and harness_logic.sh (173/0) all pass.
• **Regression tests:** tests/skills_fence_request.rs, plus unit tests in src/skill_fence.rs.

Still open
• #1002's `--safe-mode` goal-file decision, so the commit says "Part of #1002", not "Fixes".
• /doctor's skill audit (commands_dev.rs:304) re-reads the skill directories from disk, so it still lists fenced ones. That output only goes to the terminal, never to the model.

I updated the docs to match: a new entry in ARCHITECTURE.md and a paragraph in docs/src/configuration/permissions.md.
